"[2203.15556v1] Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556v1)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 29 Mar 2022]\nL9: # Title:Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models\nL10: Authors:cite5†Jordan Hoffmann , cite6†Sebastian Borgeaud , cite7†Arthur Mensch , cite8†Elena Buchatskaya , cite9†Trevor Cai , cite10†Eliza Rutherford , cite11†Diego de Las Casas , cite12†Lisa Anne Hendricks , cite13†Johannes Welbl , cite14†Aidan Clark , cite15†Tom Hennigan , cite16†Eric Noland , cite17†Katie Millican , cite18†George van den Driessche , cite19†Bogdan Damoc , cite20†Aurelia Guy , cite21†Simon Osindero , cite22†Karen Simonyan , cite23†Erich Elsen , cite24†Jack W. Rae , cite25†Oriol Vinyals , cite26†Laurent Sifre L11: View a PDF of the paper titled Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models, by Jordan Hoffmann and 21 other authors\nL12: \nL13: cite27†View PDF cite28†HTML (experimental) L14: > Abstract:We investigate the optimal model size and number of tokens for training a transformer language model under a given compute budget. We find that current large language models are significantly undertrained, a consequence of the recent focus on scaling language models whilst keeping the amount of training data constant.\nL15: By training over 400 language models ranging from 70 million to over 16 billion parameters on 5 to 500 billion tokens, we find that for compute-optimal training, the model size and the number of training tokens should be scaled equally: for every doubling of model size the number of training tokens should also be doubled. We test this hypothesis by training a predicted compute-optimal model, Chinchilla, that uses the same compute budget as Gopher but with 70B parameters and 4$\\times$ more more data.\nL16: Chinchilla uniformly and significantly outperforms Gopher (280B), GPT-3 (175B), Jurassic-1 (178B), and Megatron-Turing NLG (530B) on a large range of downstream evaluation tasks. This also means that Chinchilla uses substantially less compute for fine-tuning and inference, greatly facilitating downstream usage. As a highlight, Chinchilla reaches a state-of-the-art average accuracy of 67.5% on the MMLU benchmark, greater than a 7% improvement over Gopher.\nL17: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL18: Cite as:  | cite29†arXiv:2203.15556 [cs.CL]\nL19:    | (or cite30†arXiv:2203.15556v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL20:    | cite31†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2203.15556†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL21: ## Submission history\nL22: \nL23: From: Jordan Hoffmann [cite32†view email ]\nL24: [v1] Tue, 29 Mar 2022 13:38:03 UTC (10,143 KB)\nL25: \nL26: Full-text links:\nL27: \nL28: ## Access Paper:\nL29: \nL30: View a PDF of the paper titled Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models, by Jordan Hoffmann and 21 other authors\nL31: \nL32:   * cite27†View PDF L33:   * cite28†HTML (experimental) L34:   * cite33†TeX Source L35: \nL36: cite34†view license L37: \nL38: ### Current browse context:\nL39: \nL40: cs.CL\nL41: \nL42: cite35†< prev |   cite36†next > L43: \nL44: cite37†new | cite38†recent | cite39†2022-03 L45: \nL46: Change to browse by:\nL47: \nL48: cite40†cs L49: cite41†cs.LG L50: ### References & Citations\nL51: \nL52:   * cite42†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L53:   * cite43†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L54:   * cite44†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticschola