https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08361v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 180\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 23 Jan 2020]\nL9: # Title:Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Jared Kaplan , cite6†Sam McCandlish , cite7†Tom Henighan , cite8†Tom B. Brown , cite9†Benjamin Chess , cite10†Rewon Child , cite11†Scott Gray , cite12†Alec Radford , cite13†Jeffrey Wu , cite14†Dario Amodei L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, by Jared Kaplan and 9 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite15†View PDF cite16†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We study empirical scaling laws for language model performance on the cross-entropy loss. The loss scales as a power-law with model size, dataset size, and the amount of compute used for training, with some trends spanning more than seven orders of magnitude. Other architectural details such as network width or depth have minimal effects within a wide range. Simple equations govern the dependence of overfitting on model/dataset size and the dependence of training speed on model size.\nL17: These relationships allow us to determine the optimal allocation of a fixed compute budget. Larger models are significantly more sample-efficient, such that optimally compute-efficient training involves training very large models on a relatively modest amount of data and stopping significantly before convergence.\nL18: Comments:  | 19 pages, 15 figures\nL19: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite17†arXiv:2001.08361 [cs.LG]\nL21:    | (or cite18†arXiv:2001.08361v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL22:    | cite19†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2001.08361†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Samuel McCandlish [cite20†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Thu, 23 Jan 2020 03:59:20 UTC (1,520 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, by Jared Kaplan and 9 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite15†View PDF L35:   * cite16†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite21†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite22†view license L39: \nL40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.LG\nL43: \nL44: cite23†< prev |   cite24†next > L45: \nL46: cite25†new | cite26†recent | cite27†2020-01 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite28†cs L51: cite29†stat L52: cite30†stat.ML L53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite31†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite32†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite33†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: ### cite34†15 blog links L60: \nL61: (cite35†what is this?†info.arxiv.org )\nL62: ### cite36†DBLP†dblp.uni-trier.de - CS Bibliography\nL63: \nL64: cite37†listing†dblp.uni-trier.de | cite38†bibtex†dblp.uni-trier.de L65: \nL66: cite39†Jared Kaplan†dblp.uni-trier.de L67: cite40†Sam McCandlish†dblp.uni-trier.de L68: cite41†Tom B. Brown†dblp.uni-trier.de L69: cite42†Rewon Child†dblp.uni-trier.de L70: cite43†Scott Gray†dblp.uni-trier.de L71: \nL72: …\nL73: \nL74: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL75: \nL76: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL77: \nL78: [Button: ×]\nL79: \nL80: loading...\nL81: \nL82: Data provided by:\nL83: \nL84: ### Bookmark\nL85: \nL86: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL87: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL88: \nL89: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL90: \nL91: Bibliographic Explorer (cite44†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL92: \nL93: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL94: \nL95: Connected Papers (cite